==English==

===Etymology===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*ǵʰewd-}}
From {{inh|en|enm|refunden}}, {{m|enm|refounden}}, from {{etyl|fro|en}} {{m|fro|refondre}}, {{m|fro|refonder}}, {{m|fro|refunder||to restore; pay back}}, from {{etyl|la|en}} {{m|la|refundere}}; prefix {{m|la|re-||re-}} + {{m|la|fundere||to pour}}: compare {{cog|fr|refondre}}, {{m|fr|refonder}}. See {{m|en|fuse||to melt}}, and compare {{m|en|refound||to cast again}}, and {{m|en|refuse}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|verb}} {{enPR|rĭfŭnd'}}, {{IPA|en|/ɹɪˈfʌnd/}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-refund-verb.ogg|Audio (US)}}
* {{a|noun}} {{enPR|rē'fŭnd'}}, {{IPA|en|/ˈɹiːfʌnd/}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-refund-noun.ogg|Audio (US)}}
* {{rhymes|en|ʌnd}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|transitive}} To return ([[money]]) to (someone); to [[reimburse]].
#: ''If you find this computer for sale anywhere at a lower price, we'll '''refund''' you the difference.''
#* {{RQ:L'Estrange Fables of Aesop|passage=A Governor, that had Pillag'd the People, was {{...}} sentenc'd to '''Refund''' what he had Wrongfully Taken.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|obsolete}} To [[supply]] again with funds.
#: ''to '''refund''' a railroad loan''
# {{lb|en|transitive|obsolete|rare}} To [[pour]] back.
#* '''1660''', {{w|John Ray}}, ''The Wisdom of God Manifested in the Works of the Creation''
#*: Were the humours of the eye tinctured with any colour, they would '''refund''' that colour upon the object.

